            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "The singular value decomposition: writes any matrix as three simpler ones, U, S and Vh, so that the matrix equals U times diag(S) times Vh. Vh turns the input into a new set of axes, S stretches along each of them, and U turns the result into place.\n\nThe singular values S, largest first, say how much the matrix acts along each of its independent directions. A matrix with one large value and the rest tiny is nearly one-dimensional - everything in it lies along one line - and keeping only the large values gives the best possible simpler version of it. That is the idea behind principal components, noise reduction, finding the best-fitting rotation between two sets of points, and compressing a set of poses into a few basic movements.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.linalg.svd\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nmatrix:\nAny 2-D matrix (or a stack of them). Every input is decomposed and sent.\n\nfull matrices:\nAn option, under the node. Off (the default) gives the compact form, with no more columns in U (rows in Vh) than there are singular values. On gives square U and Vh.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nU:\nThe output directions, one column per singular value.\n\nsingular values:\nHow strongly the matrix acts along each direction, largest first, never negative.\n\nVh:\nThe input directions, one row per singular value. Sent first, then the singular values, then U.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe 3 x 2 matrix 1 2 / 3 4 / 5 6 has singular values about 9.53 and 0.51. The first is nineteen times the second: its rows nearly lie on one line, and it is almost a one-dimensional matrix. U and Vh show the directions; the round node only tidies the values for reading.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.eigh does the same for a symmetric matrix such as a covariance. np.linalg.pinv and np.linalg.lstsq are built on the SVD. np.linalg.matrix_rank counts the singular values that are not essentially zero. t.linalg.svd is the PyTorch version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1050,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "The singular value decomposition: writes any matrix as three simpler ones, U, S and Vh, so that the matrix equals U times diag(S) times Vh. Vh turns the input into a new set of axes, S stretches along each of them, and U turns the result into place.\n\nThe singular values S, largest first, say how much the matrix acts along each of its independent directions. A matrix with one large value and the rest tiny is nearly one-dimensional - everything in it lies along one line - and keeping only the large values gives the best possible simpler version of it. That is the idea behind principal components, noise reduction, finding the best-fitting rotation between two sets of points, and compressing a set of poses into a few basic movements.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.linalg.svd\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nmatrix:\nAny 2-D matrix (or a stack of them). Every input is decomposed and sent.\n\nfull matrices:\nAn option, under the node. Off (the default) gives the compact form, with no more columns in U (rows in Vh) than there are singular values. On gives square U and Vh.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nU:\nThe output directions, one column per singular value.\n\nsingular values:\nHow strongly the matrix acts along each direction, largest first, never negative.\n\nVh:\nThe input directions, one row per singular value. Sent first, then the singular values, then U.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe 3 x 2 matrix 1 2 / 3 4 / 5 6 has singular values about 9.53 and 0.51. The first is nineteen times the second: its rows nearly lie on one line, and it is almost a one-dimensional matrix. U and Vh show the directions; the round node only tidies the values for reading.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.eigh does the same for a symmetric matrix such as a covariance. np.linalg.pinv and np.linalg.lstsq are built on the SVD. np.linalg.matrix_rank counts the singular values that are not essentially zero. t.linalg.svd is the PyTorch version."
        },
